{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Phase 1 evidence capture (AC1) — first-hand read-only CloudWatch + in-repo/runtime evidence\n\nDebug worktree `debug/3a52-root-disk-pressure` off `origin/main` (0b851c8103). All AWS calls\nbelow are read-only `cloudwatch:GetMetricStatistics` / `ListMetrics`. **No host access was\nused** — `ssm:SendCommand` is blocked in this session, so journald/`du` evidence is NOT part\nof this record (see \"What still needs a host operation\" at the end).\n\n### 1. The reclaim is not a prune — it is a return to a FIXED floor\n\n`rebar/host:root_disk_used_percent`, 5-min samples, 2026-08-15 → 2026-08-19: **23 drops of\n≥5 percentage points, and every one lands on 48%** (two on 49%/51%). Drop sizes range\n1.5 GiB → 12.6 GiB. The incident drop is `90% @22:00 → 48% @22:05` = 12.6 GiB.\n\nA `docker builder prune --keep-storage 5GB` + `docker image prune -f` leaves a *variable*\nresidue that depends on what was reclaimable at that moment. It cannot produce an identical\n48% floor across 23 independent events of wildly different sizes. A constant floor is the\nsignature of an **ephemeral working set being destroyed wholesale**, returning the disk to\nits baseline.\n\n### 2. Growth tracks REVIEW volume, not deploy volume\n\n`review_bot_llm_total_tokens` was sustained 0.8M–3.0M per 5 min from 20:35 straight through\n22:30 — reviews ran continuously *before, during and after* the drop. Root-disk growth rate\nwas ~4.4 GiB/h **before** the drop (69%→90% over 85 min) and ~4.3 GiB/h **after** it\n(48%→54% over 25 min). Same rate, same driver, uninterrupted by the reclaim.\n\n### 3. The deferral episode did not cover the growth window\n\n`deploy_deferrals`: **0** in every 5-min bucket from 20:30 through 21:50, then 2 / 3 / 1 at\n21:55 / 22:00 / 22:05. The disk crossed `DISK_PRESSURE_PCT` (80%) at **21:25** and the 85%\nalarm floor at **21:35** — 20–30 minutes *before* the first deferral. So the deferral path\ncannot be what suppressed reclaim during the climb, and `deploy_errors` = `voter_errors` =\n`review_interrupts` = **0** throughout (no backoff, no failed deploy, no ENOSPC).\n\n### 4. Both docker-prune hypotheses are therefore weakened, and the disk-consumer question moves\n\n`infra/scripts/docker-compose.yml`'s `review-bot` service mounts **only**\n`./rebar-bot-signing-key` (ro), `gerrit_reviewbot` → `/var/gerrit/site/reviewbot`, and\n`gerrit_reviewbot_tickets` → `/var/gerrit/site/reviewbot-tickets` — both bind-backed onto the\n**separate 50-GiB `/var/gerrit` EBS volume**. There is no `tmpfs` and no other mount, and\n`REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR` is not set in the compose environment. So everything else the bot writes\nat runtime lands on the **container writable layer**, i.e. `/var/lib/docker` on the 30-GiB\n**root** volume — and those bytes are neither buildkit cache nor a dangling image, so\n`prune_docker_caches` **cannot reclaim them at all** while the container is running.\n\n`src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:113` `store_root()` =\n`config.resolve_gate_tmpdir() or tempfile.gettempdir()` + store dirname. Verified at runtime\nwith `REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR` unset: it resolves under `tempfile.gettempdir()`, i.e. `/tmp` inside\nthe container.\n\n### 5. RUNTIME EXPERIMENT (AC2 discriminator, part 1): the snapshot janitor cannot fire before the alarm\n\nDrove the **real** `rebar._snapshot.janitor.run_gc()` with injected free-space values for a\n30-GiB root (5 warm, out-of-grace, not-cold entries — the bot's steady state under continuous\nreview load):\n\n| root used | free injected | evicted | reclaimed |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 71% (pre-incident) | 8.70 GiB | **0** | 0 |\n| 80% (`DISK_PRESSURE_PCT`) | 6.00 GiB | **0** | 0 |\n| **85% (CloudWatch ALARM floor)** | 4.50 GiB | **0** | 0 |\n| **90% (observed incident peak)** | 3.00 GiB | **0** | 0 |\n| 94% (past the watermark) | 1.80 GiB | 5 | 20480 |\n\n`src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py:53` `DEFAULT_FREE_WATERMARK_BYTES = 2 * 1024**3`. On a 30-GiB\nroot that watermark is only crossed at **93.3% used** — strictly *above* the 85% alarm floor\nand above the 90% peak actually reached. **The alarm necessarily fires before snapshot GC can\never engage.** This is hypothesis 1 of the ticket, now confirmed dynamically rather than by\nreading the constant.\n\n### 6. The health dip is the same event, caught by sampling luck\n\n`reviewbot_healthy` (dimension `InstanceId=i-00880b2c7f13527c5`) was 0 in exactly **two**\n5-min buckets across 2026-08-15→19; one of them is **2026-08-17 22:05** — the same bucket as\nthe 90%→48% drop. Only 1 of the 23 reclaim events shows a health dip because the host probe\npolls every 5 minutes and the recreation window is seconds long. No separate cause is needed\nfor the \"review-bot drops unhealthy\" symptom.\n\n### Working account (not yet fully proven)\n\nOne mechanism explains every symptom: the review-bot's **ephemeral per-review working set**\nlives on the container writable layer (30-GiB root) because no volume covers it; it is\ninvisible to `prune_docker_caches`; the snapshot janitor's 2-GiB-free watermark sits below the\nalarm floor so GC never engages; and the bytes are freed only when a deploy **recreates** the\ncontainer — hence the constant 48% floor and the coincident health dip.\n\n### What still needs a host operation (blocked here)\n\n`ssm:SendCommand` is denied in this session, so I could not run the bounded read-only host\nprobe that would name the **dominant** consumer directly. The exact command wanted is a\nread-only `AWS-RunShellScript` on `i-00880b2c7f13527c5`:\n`df -h /`; `docker system df -v`; `docker ps --size`; `du -xh --max-depth=2 /tmp` **inside the\nreview-bot container**; and\n`journalctl -u rebar-autodeploy.service --since \"2026-08-18 03:30\" --until \"2026-08-18 06:00\"`\nplus a count of `AUTODEPLOY_DISK_PRESSURE` markers (that marker is deliberately not published\nas a CloudWatch metric — story 28f9 scoped it out — so journald is its only reader).\n`docker ps --size` alone would settle it: it prints each running container's writable-layer\nsize.\n\nrebar-debug Phase 1, Stage 3 continues; no repair has been attempted."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787172452151978001,"uuid":"94e6ef2a-320c-460f-8e8f-bf9f8d8611e3"}